# Overlap check

Before building any new Feature component, search
src/primitives, src/atoms, src/molecules, and src/organisms
for overlap — an existing component that already covers the
need (same job, even if the visual treatment differs).

If a match exists → compose or override it (see below).
Do not build a parallel Feature-local copy.

If nothing matches → still apply the placement test
before creating a new file.

Report what you searched and what you found (or that
nothing matched) before writing new component code.

## Modifying a Primitive/Atom from a Feature

When a Feature component needs to modify a Primitive/Atom's
appearance (padding, hover color, size, etc.), the modification must
happen one of these ways, in order of preference:

1. className / style prop override at the call site, using
   Foundations tokens — never hardcoded values.
2. A new variant/prop added to the primitive itself, if the change
   is plausibly reusable by other features — not feature-specific
   conditionals baked into the primitive.
3. Composition — wrap the primitive rather than modifying it.

NEVER copy a primitive's source into a feature folder and edit the
copy. If a needed change can't be expressed via 1-3, stop and report
why before building a duplicate.

Report which approach was used and why, whenever a primitive is
touched or overridden while building a feature.

## Placement test for new components

Before creating any new component file while working in
src/features/, ask: "Would this make sense reused somewhere else,
with different content, unrelated to this feature?"

YES → belongs in src/atoms, src/molecules, or src/organisms
(whichever tier fits its complexity), not the feature folder.

NO → stays in src/features/<feature-name>/, built from existing
primitives/atoms/molecules via composition.

State which answer applies and why before deciding where a new
file goes.

## Conflicting Figma variants

Figma variants of a single component are not always different
states — sometimes they're conflicting structural explorations from
before a decision was made. When variants disagree on structure (not
just visual state), do not average them or guess which is correct.
The person will designate the authoritative variant explicitly. Build
only that one.
